<resources>

    <!-- ===== Field help: the text of each field's i button in the channel editor (also shown by SignalDeck Web) ===== -->
    <!-- Help card of a channel editor field (the i button), also on SignalDeck Web. Paragraph. -->
    <string name="channel_editor_h_notes">Anything worth remembering about this channel: who uses it, the repeater\'s owner or club, linking, hours, how to check in. It can go with the channel when you share it (Share channels → Channel notes).</string>
    <!-- Help card of a channel editor field (the i button), also on SignalDeck Web. Paragraph. %1$d is the longest name the radio takes (characters). -->
    <string name="channel_editor_h_name">Shown on the intercom, in the channel list and in voice announcements. Up to %1$d characters; the counter under the field shows how many are left. The radio itself never receives the name. Codeplug tools such as OpenGD77 CPS keep shorter names (16 characters), so a long name may be cut short in a backup opened there.</string>
    <!-- Help card of a channel editor field (the i button), also on SignalDeck Web. Paragraph. -->
    <string name="channel_editor_h_type">Digital (DMR) channels carry voice and text as data and need a colour code, a time slot and a call target. Analog channels are classic FM. M17 channels are wide FM with M17 layered on them: a destination callsign (or everyone) and a channel access number. Switching shows the settings that apply.</string>
    <!-- Help card of a channel editor field (the i button), also on SignalDeck Web. Paragraph. -->
    <string name="channel_editor_h_m17">Who this channel calls. @ALL is everyone. A callsign calls that station. Pick from contacts uses a contact that has a callsign. Your own callsign, the one you transmit from, is the station callsign in APRS settings.</string>
    <!-- Help card of a channel editor field (the i button), also on SignalDeck Web. Paragraph. -->
    <string name="channel_editor_h_can">Channel access number, 0 to 15. Radios only hear each other when this matches. 0 is the usual choice.</string>
    <!-- Help card of a channel editor field (the i button), also on SignalDeck Web. Paragraph. %1$d is the longest name the radio takes (characters). -->
    <string name="channel_editor_h_tx">The frequency you transmit on, in MHz. On a simplex channel it is the same as the receive frequency; on a repeater channel it is the repeater\'s input (for example +5 MHz on UHF GMRS, or ±0.6 MHz on 2 m). Left empty, it is the receive frequency (simplex). This radio tunes %1$d.</string>
    <!-- Help card of a channel editor field (the i button), also on SignalDeck Web. Paragraph. %1$d is the longest name the radio takes (characters). -->
    <string name="channel_editor_h_rx">The frequency you listen on, in MHz. On a repeater channel it is the repeater\'s output. This radio tunes %1$d; other frequencies (220 MHz, for example) cannot be saved.</string>
    <!-- Help card of a channel editor field (the i button), also on SignalDeck Web. Paragraph. -->
    <string name="channel_editor_h_pos">Optional position of the station or repeater this channel reaches. With it the intercom shows the place name and its distance and bearing from you. Use my position fills in the phone\'s last known location.</string>
    <!-- Help card of a channel editor field (the i button), also on SignalDeck Web. Paragraph. -->
    <string name="channel_editor_h_rxonly">Receive only: this channel never transmits. Talk, the side key, the voice keyer, beacons, replies and every other transmission are refused while you are on it (a listening post, a channel you are not licensed for, a frequency that should stay quiet). NOAA weather, marine and railroad channels are always receive only, whatever this says.</string>
    <!-- Help card of a channel editor field (the i button), also on SignalDeck Web. Paragraph. -->
    <string name="channel_editor_h_power">Transmit power. High reaches further and uses more battery; Low is enough for nearby radios. Some services limit power (FRS, for example).</string>
    <!-- Help card of a channel editor field (the i button), also on SignalDeck Web. Paragraph. -->
    <string name="channel_editor_h_band">Channel width: Wide (25 kHz) or Narrow (12.5 kHz). Use what the other radios or the repeater use. On VHF (below 300 MHz) this radio is always set to Wide: Narrow makes its receiver deaf there.</string>
    <!-- Help card of a channel editor field (the i button), also on SignalDeck Web. Paragraph. -->
    <string name="channel_editor_h_relay">The radio\'s relay-disconnection setting. Normal (recommended): the radio stays connected to the repeater and transmits normally. Disconnect: the radio waits for repeater confirmation and may not transmit at all; use it only if your network asks for it.</string>
    <!-- Help card of a channel editor field (the i button), also on SignalDeck Web. Paragraph. -->
    <string name="channel_editor_h_cc">DMR\'s equivalent of a squelch tone, 0 to 15. Your radio and the repeater (or the other radios on a simplex channel) must use the same colour code, or you will not hear each other.</string>
    <!-- Help card of a channel editor field (the i button), also on SignalDeck Web. Paragraph. -->
    <string name="channel_editor_h_mode">Direct: radio to radio (simplex). Double slot: through a DMR repeater, which carries two time slots at once. Use Double slot for repeater channels.</string>
    <!-- Help card of a channel editor field (the i button), also on SignalDeck Web. Paragraph. -->
    <string name="channel_editor_h_noslot">Time slot: chosen by this phone\'s radio module. Its channel setting has no slot, so SignalDeck cannot pick TS1 or TS2 here, and repeater (double slot) channels may not work. Simplex DMR with other radios is the safe use.</string>
    <!-- Help card of a channel editor field (the i button), also on SignalDeck Web. Paragraph. -->
    <string name="channel_editor_h_slot">A DMR repeater carries two separate conversations, time slot 1 and 2. Use the slot your talkgroup is on (the repeater\'s listing says). On a simplex channel every radio must use the same slot, usually TS1.</string>
    <!-- Help card of a channel editor field (the i button), also on SignalDeck Web. Paragraph. -->
    <string name="channel_editor_h_ctype">Who you call: Private (one DMR ID), Group (a talkgroup) or All (every radio). On this radio a channel set to All reports callers as ID 16777215 and does not pass received audio, so use Group for normal talkgroup traffic.</string>
    <!-- Help card of a channel editor field (the i button), also on SignalDeck Web. Paragraph. -->
    <string name="channel_editor_h_callfrom">Type the call number yourself, or pick it from your contacts: group contacts (talkgroups) for a group call, private contacts (DMR IDs) for a private call. Either way the channel stores the number; add talkgroups on the Contacts page with type Group. On the intercom, a long-press on CALL picks another talkgroup for a while without editing the channel.</string>
    <!-- Help card of a channel editor field (the i button), also on SignalDeck Web. Paragraph. -->
    <string name="channel_editor_h_callpick">The contact this channel calls. Tap to choose another one; the list follows the contact type above.</string>
    <!-- Help card of a channel editor field (the i button), also on SignalDeck Web. Paragraph. -->
    <string name="channel_editor_h_call">The DMR ID (private call) or talkgroup number (group call) you transmit to. For a group call it is added to the RX group list when you save, because the radio only passes group calls whose talkgroup is in that list.</string>
    <!-- Help card of a channel editor field (the i button), also on SignalDeck Web. Paragraph. -->
    <string name="channel_editor_h_groups">Talkgroups you want to hear on this channel, separated by commas, up to 32. The radio passes a group call only if its talkgroup is in this list. Private calls to your own DMR ID always pass, and MON on the intercom passes every call the radio can decode. Talkgroup lists below inserts a saved list (the module\'s lists come over from the stock app) or saves this one under a name.</string>
    <!-- Help card of a channel editor field (the i button), also on SignalDeck Web. Paragraph. -->
    <string name="channel_editor_h_enc">The radio\'s own DMR privacy scrambling with an 8-character key. Only radios using the same scheme and the same key hear you; most other DMR radios cannot. Encryption is not allowed on amateur frequencies.</string>
    <!-- Help card of a channel editor field (the i button), also on SignalDeck Web. Paragraph. -->
    <string name="channel_editor_h_key">Exactly 8 characters, the same on every radio in the group. Used only when Encrypt is Enabled.</string>
    <!-- Help card of a channel editor field (the i button), also on SignalDeck Web. Paragraph. -->
    <string name="channel_editor_h_int">DMR transmit interrupt. ON allows a call in progress to be interrupted (for example by the radio you are calling) on radios that support it; when it is refused the radio reports that the called device does not allow interruption. OFF is the stock setting.</string>
    <!-- Help card of a channel editor field (the i button), also on SignalDeck Web. Paragraph. -->
    <string name="channel_editor_h_sq">The radio\'s own squelch, used when Soft SQ is off for this channel. 0 keeps it fully open: you hear static and the radio reports a reception all the time. 1 to 9 close it; on this radio they behave as one threshold, about −90 dBm.</string>
    <!-- Help card of a channel editor field (the i button), also on SignalDeck Web. Paragraph. -->
    <string name="channel_editor_h_softsq">Soft SQ holds the radio\'s squelch open and lets the app decide what you hear: the channel\'s CTCSS tone when it has one, otherwise a noise squelch that tells speech from static. It catches weaker stations than the radio\'s own squelch. The SOFT SQ key on the intercom switches it too.</string>
    <!-- Help card of a channel editor field (the i button), also on SignalDeck Web. Paragraph. -->
    <string name="channel_editor_h_softsq_lvl">How strict Soft SQ is, from 1 (lenient: opens on weaker, noisier signals) to 9 (strict: only clean signals). Same as the slider on the intercom.</string>
    <!-- Help card of a channel editor field (the i button), also on SignalDeck Web. Paragraph. -->
    <string name="channel_editor_h_maps">Open Maps shows this position (or yours) in your maps app. Find the place there and use Share → SignalDeck, or copy its coordinates or link and come back to tap Paste. Paste reads coordinates, geo: links and Google, Apple or OpenStreetMap links from the clipboard, following short share links.</string>
    <!-- Help card of a channel editor field (the i button), also on SignalDeck Web. Paragraph. -->
    <string name="channel_editor_h_txt">A sub-audible tone sent with your transmission: CTCSS (a low tone, 62.5 to 254.1 Hz) or DCS (a digital code). Repeaters and radios that use tone squelch only open for the right one; a repeater\'s listing gives its access tone.</string>
    <!-- Help card of a channel editor field (the i button), also on SignalDeck Web. Paragraph. -->
    <string name="channel_editor_h_txc">The CTCSS frequency or DCS code to send. Tap to pick from the list.</string>
    <!-- Help card of a channel editor field (the i button), also on SignalDeck Web. Paragraph. -->
    <string name="channel_editor_h_rxt">Only signals carrying this tone open the squelch, so other users on the frequency stay quiet. With Soft SQ on, the app checks the CTCSS tone itself; DCS is checked by the radio.</string>
    <!-- Help card of a channel editor field (the i button), also on SignalDeck Web. Paragraph. -->
    <string name="channel_editor_h_rxc">The CTCSS frequency or DCS code to listen for. Tap to pick from the list.</string>

